# CAPS S&C UI Style Guide

All screens—including future implementations of unfinished screens (`src/screens/StrengthTrainingScreen.tsx`, `src/screens/SkillScreen.tsx`, and the `Recovery` / `Progress` tabs in `src/screens/HomeScreen.tsx`)—must strictly follow this style guide.

## 1. Core Design Philosophy

- **High-Contrast, Minimalist, Data-Dense**: Deep carbon backgrounds (`#060709` dark / `#F3F4F6` light), crisp elevated card surfaces (`#101216` dark / `#FFFFFF` light), secondary muted wells (`#181B22` dark / `#E5E7EB` light), high-contrast primary text (`#FAFAFC` dark / `#090A0D` light), and explicit `1px` structural borders (`border` and `borderStrong`).
- **Card-by-Card Layout**: Group every functional domain or input cluster into its own bordered card (`borderRadius: theme.radius.lg` = `16`, `borderWidth: 1`, `padding: theme.spacing.lg` = `16`, `gap: theme.spacing.md` = `12`). Separate cards vertically by `12px–16px`.
- **Card Headers**: Every card starts with a compact header row containing an uppercase tracking label (`fontSize: 11`, `fontWeight: "700"`, `letterSpacing: 0.8`, `color: theme.colors.textMuted`) and an optional right-aligned tabular metric badge or status pill.
- **Tabular Numerals**: Any number, timer, weight, macro, RPE, distance, or counter must include `fontVariant: ["tabular-nums"]` to prevent layout jitter.
- **Shared Interaction Contracts**: Reuse `WorkflowHeader`, `WorkflowFooter`, `ScreenTitleCard`, and `LogTimeChanger` across screens so navigation and log-time entry remain uniform. Keep domain-specific form composition local; avoid duplicate implementations of the same interaction.

## 2. Minimal Modals Policy

- **Screens and Dropdowns by Default**: Use screens or dropdowns for forms and menus. Do not introduce a modal unless explicitly requested or covered by an existing exception.
- **Log Time Exception**: `LogTimeChanger` opens the explicitly requested modal with scrollable hour and minute columns. Confirmation dialogs and the documented conditioning overlays remain allowed.
- **Biometric Exceptions (23 November 2026)**: Age/date of birth, sex, height, and weight use compact editing modals from the interactive Biometric Overview. Reuse one modal foundation. Weight logging and history editing everywhere use the same actual `WeightLogModal`, including Home Quick Log; do not implement it as a screen overlay. Keep its date/time control inside that modal.

## 3. Simple Inputs & Large Touch Targets

- **Minimum Touch Target**: Every interactive button, chip, or toggle must have at least `minHeight: 48` and `minWidth: 48` (`52px` height for primary user flow CTAs).
- **Weight and Load**: Use one labeled decimal text box with the current kg/lb unit for every bodyweight, load, resistance, assistance, or added-weight input. Never add fine/coarse increment buttons or weight preset strips. Preserve canonical kg conversion and existing validation; allow an empty draft while typing without saving invalid values.
- **Numeric Inputs (24 November 2026 user correction)**: Use direct text boxes for numeric parameters. Do not introduce numeric increment/decrement buttons or numeric preset strips. This includes work/rest seconds, sets, reps, RPE, intervals and rounds. Date and timeframe chevrons remain navigation controls. Reuse TrainingNumberField for training numeric forms; preserve raw drafts and validate on the action.
- **Strength Exercise Table (26 November 2026 user request)**: Sets, Rep Ranges, Targeted RPE and Targeted RIR scroll directly in their cells. Rep Ranges also opens an anchored overlay on tap. Exercise search uses an anchored overlay. Completed set rows expand inline and use direct numeric inputs for actual reps, RPE and RIR. Planned exercises show exercise-level targets only.
- **Dates and Personal Measurements**: Use scrollable date-of-birth and height pickers, direct weight input, and icon-only chevrons for previous/next day or month. Do not expose numeric step labels for these interactions.
- **Keyboard Access**: Give every field an accessible label and suitable keyboard, preserve drafts on errors, and keep primary actions reachable above the keyboard. AppNavigator owns normal screen avoidance (iOS padding, Android height); do not add duplicate screen-level layers. Actual native modals retain their separately verified padding behavior. Use `keyboardShouldPersistTaps="handled"` on form scroll views.

## 4. User Flow Buttons

- **Primary Action**: Background `theme.colors.tertiary`, text `readableTextColor(theme.colors.tertiary)`, `minHeight: 52`, `borderRadius: 12`, `fontWeight: "700"`.
- **Secondary Action**: Background `theme.colors.surfaceMuted`, border `1px solid theme.colors.borderStrong`, text `theme.colors.text`, `minHeight: 48`, `borderRadius: 12`, `fontWeight: "700"`.
- **Quiet / Destructive Action**: Transparent or tinted danger fill (`rgba(211, 21, 22, 0.14)`), border `1px solid`, `minHeight: 48`, `borderRadius: 12`.

## 5. Uniform Animations

- Use `react-native-reanimated` with `ReduceMotion.System`.
- **Inline Expanders & Segmented Controls**: `duration: 220`, `easing: Easing.out(Easing.cubic)`.
- **Stack / Sheet Transitions**: `duration: 280`, `easing: Easing.out(Easing.cubic)`.
- **Press Feedback**: Immediate opacity change (`pressedOpacity = 0.62`) paired with sensory feedback.

## 6. Audio Cues & Haptic Feedback

- Route user interactions through `src/utils/feedback.ts`:
  - `feedbackSelection()` / `feedbackStep("up" | "down")`: Light haptic tick + subtle acoustic click on tab changes, chip selections, and date navigation.
  - `feedbackConfirm()`: Medium/Success haptic + confirmation chime when saving, logging, or completing a flow.
  - `feedbackPhaseCue()` / `feedbackCountdownTick()`: Strong haptic + high-visibility acoustic beep on workout runner countdowns (3-2-1) and work/recovery phase transitions.
  - `feedbackWarn()`: Warning/Error haptic + low tone on destructive confirmations or validation errors.

## 7. Unfinished Screens Mandate

- Do not modify `src/screens/StrengthTrainingScreen.tsx`, `src/screens/SkillScreen.tsx`, or the `Recovery` / `Progress` placeholder panels in `src/screens/HomeScreen.tsx` until requested.
- When building those features in the future, follow the current simple-input, shared-navigation, minimal-modal, high-contrast conventions.

## 8. Uniform Logging and Subscreen Navigation (20 November 2026)

- Apply these contracts across Home, Nutrition, Strength, Conditioning, and Skills Training, including every nested destination. The five main screens retain their main-screen title cards.
- Every subscreen has a dedicated fixed top bar and bottom action bar, with scrolling content between them. Account for the phone status bar / cutout at the top exactly once. Existing bottom tabs own their system bottom inset; a standalone flow must provide its own bottom safe area.
- Center the subscreen title. Put an X at the left that exits the entire feature branch to its main screen, regardless of nesting depth. Route X through existing unsaved-change, active-session, and save-pending guards. Never wire X to a one-level pop.
- **Settings exception (23 November 2026)**: Settings, Account Information, and Athlete Information use a left menu icon opening the existing sidebar, with no global log-time control. The sidebar X exits settings to the remembered main tab through existing guards. This exception does not change training or other primary workflow headers.
- Put the compact `LogTimeChanger` at the right of the top bar when a log timestamp applies. Show only a time icon and selected time on the trigger. Sleep may show two labeled compact triggers in this same right-hand area for Bed and Wake.
- No log-time cards, inline expanders, hour presets, Now buttons, or minute/hour increment buttons. No increment buttons flanking a clock trigger.
- The picker modal title is **Log Time**. Directly below it, put minimalist previous/next day arrows around **{Day} {Mon}, {Year}** (for example **22 Nov, 2026**). Use independently scrollable hour and minute columns. Cancel leaves the timestamp unchanged; Done applies it. Preserve each caller's date bounds and timestamp/duration logic.
- The bottom bar's left action is **Cancel** on the first subscreen only, returning to the main screen. At every deeper level it is **Back**, returning one screen while preserving the parent draft. Use actual branch depth, including nested editors and detail routes.
- Keep existing Save, Logged, Log, Delete, template, timer, and other primary actions alongside navigation. Preserve their underlying logic unless it conflicts with the user's requirements. Do not bury these actions at the end of scrolling content.
- Review every new or modified flow for safe-area access, long titles, large text, keyboard access, X exit from deepest nesting, Cancel at depth one, Back at deeper levels, and save failure recovery.

## 9. Minimal copy (22 November 2026)

- Keep the default UI to titles, values, units, labels, controls and short actionable errors. Remove generic screen subtitles, navigation instructions, repeated definitions and supporting paragraphs.
- Under each Recovery metric, show only one short definition or the concise requirement to obtain it. Use `--` for missing values; do not repeat an unavailable status label. Keep the experimental designation in the ORI title.
- Keep formulas, model versions, coverage diagnostics, source IDs and sync internals in code/documentation and stored provenance, not as default card or tooltip paragraphs. Source choices remain accessible under health management. Never change calculations or fabricate readings to simplify display.
- Progress graph categories use the Choose graph overlay dropdown. Preserve the existing timeframe selector, accessible 48px controls, legend toggles, source-separated series and synchronized date cursor. Tooltips show date, metric values and units; aggregates need only a short Mean label.
- Tighten redundant card gaps, stacked status rows and empty charts while keeping readable text, safe areas and touch targets. Preserve field anchors, dates/timezones needed to interpret answers, actual error/retry actions and discard/delete confirmations.

## 10. Questionnaire and Progress interaction update (22 November 2026)

- Questionnaire sleep uses two regular minute-resolution LogTimeChanger controls, calculated total interval/estimated sleep and no sleep timeline. Keep actual dates/timezones, validation and DST disambiguation.
- Rating controls are full-width numbered/notched lines without visible +/- buttons. New rating defaults are 5; awakenings defaults 0 with a 10+ endpoint. Preserve the 10+ lower-bound marker and legacy exact counts.
- Questionnaire history opens a dedicated screen. Editor Back/save returns to history, X exits the branch, and dirty/save guards still apply. Model inputs/weights are versioned; see docs/questionnaire-sri-v2.md.
- Render full dates as 22 Nov, 2026 using the shared display formatter. Preserve ISO storage/query keys.
- Choose graph overlays the screen. Timeframe selection resets to the current period. Remove data-point arrow navigation; graphs/accessibility actions select dates.
- Below-chart values are the toggle legend and remain available when a series is hidden. Wellness plots show higher-is-better directions without modifying raw stored answers.
- Fixed 0–10 axes use steps 2; 0–100 uses 10. Awakening axes start 0; sleep spans at least 0–16 hours and expands for actual larger values. Fit X labels to width/font scaling and align synchronized plot insets.
